TI  - Detection of CITES-listed hoodia species and extracts thereof in herbal 
      supplements using LC-HRMS/MS: validation study and qPCR method comparison.
PG  - 683-694
LID - 10.1080/19440049.2026.2645431 [doi]
AB  - This study describes the validation of a approach to detect in herbal supplements 
      regulated Hoodia species or their extracts, targeting the oxypregnane steroidal 
      glycoside P57AS3, using liquid chromatography coupled to tandem mass spectrometry 
      with a high-resolution acute mass detection (LC-HRMS/MS). The method was 
      validated by assessing the selectivity and specificity by analysing other related 
      Apocynaceae species, particularly Stapeliinae subtribe members, and various 
      plants commonly found in dietary supplements promoting weight loss. The limit of 
      detection (LOD), equal to 8 microg P57AS3 per gram powder, matrix effects, and the 
      ratio of false positives (0%) were determined. Next, the analytical sensitivity 
      of the LC-HRMS/MS methodology was compared to a recently developed quantitative 
      PCR (qPCR) method by comparing the results obtained from 20 commercial herbal 
      supplements, often originating from rogue internet pharmacies, that either 
      claimed or were reported to contain Hoodia sp. material or extracts thereof. The 
      study results highlight the complementary nature of LC-HRMS/MS and qPCR. While 
      qPCR provides high molecular sensitivity for powdered material, LC-HRMS/MS 
      delivers chemical confirmation and detection of metabolites when DNA is absent or 
      degraded. The integration of both techniques creates a comprehensive detection 
      framework, enhancing accuracy, reliability, and confidence in the identification 
      of regulated plant material across different and complex herbal matrices and 
      mixtures thereof.
