VI  - 286
DP  - 2025 Oct 15
TI  - Ultrasensitive quantification of aflatoxin B(1) via nanobody phage -mediated 
      quantitative PCR and digital PCR for agro-products.
PG  - 117622
LID - S0956-5663(25)00496-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.bios.2025.117622 [doi]
AB  - Phage display-mediated immuno-polymerase chain reaction (PD-IPCR) combines the 
      strengths of phage display technology and immuno-PCR. The link between genotype 
      and phenotype has been established by displaying antibody fragments on the 
      surface of phage particles, allowing signal transmission. In this study, we 
      utilized aflatoxin B(1) (AFB(1)) as a model system to investigate the efficacy of 
      PD-IPCR. Signal amplification was achieved using both real-time fluorescence 
      quantitative PCR (qPCR) and a custom-made droplet digital PCR (dPCR) system. 
      Under optimized conditions, the assay using dPCR achieved a limit of detection of 
      0.015 ng/mL, a 6-fold improvement over qPCR. The validation of our method using 
      major agricultural produce, such as corn, flour, and soy sauce, shows excellent 
      recoveries ranging from 85.77 % to 108.28 %, with a total assay time of less than 
      2 h. Furthermore, we compared our results with a high-performance liquid 
      chromatography (HPLC) method for AFB(1) content in corn and flour, revealing 
      consistent outcomes (r = 0.9847). These results demonstrate the potential of 
      nanobody-based PD-IPCR as a reliable tool for detecting mycotoxins.
